# What each profile's own SUnit suites score, as of the last time somebody
# looked.  Read by tests/run_profiles.sh, which `make test` runs.
#
# This is a RATCHET, not a target.  The rule is that neither number may fall:
# a suite that ran 633 tests must still run 633, and a suite that passed 633
# must still pass 633.  Both directions matter, and the first one matters
# more than it looks -- a package whose tests stop being FOUND reports a
# perfect score, and that is exactly the shape of the failure this file
# exists to catch.  The Chronology suites spent a while reporting nothing at
# all because four classes deadlocked the runner, and nothing noticed.
#
# Improving a number fails too, with a message saying so.  That is
# deliberate: an unrecorded improvement is an improvement nothing is
# protecting, and the next regression silently spends it.  Edit the line in
# the same commit that earns it.
#
# One of these is not at 100%, and is recorded honestly rather than excluded:
#
#   pharo-weak           12/32 -- finalization, which the object memory does
#                                 not implement yet; this is Phase F work.
#                                 The twenty errors are all one fault --
#                                 `division by 0' out of WeakSet -- and are
#                                 the next thing to look at rather than a
#                                 standing exemption.
#
# pharo-announcements is at 43/43 with WeakAnnouncerTest excluded: it tests
# WEAK announcements, and the profile says so.
#
# pharo-collections runs Pharo's Set, Dictionary, IdentityDictionary, Bag and
# MethodDictionary against the package's OWN tests, 469 of them, and all 469
# pass.  It was 115 when they were first wired in, and the grind from there
# found faults in this system rather than in the tests: a block with a
# trailing period answered nil, Object>>copy did not send postCopy so every
# copy of a Pharo collection shared its storage, becomeForward: did not
# exist, and the collector refused ephemerons.  Two of those four were
# compiler and object-memory bugs that had nothing to do with collections.
#
# A profile with no SUnit at all (bluebook) is not listed: it has no
# TestCase, so there is nothing to ask it.
#
# profile               run  passed
st2026                  12   12
pharo-announcements     43   43
pharo-time              633  633
pharo-weak              32   12
pharo-collections       469  469
